  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "Etiqueta de Caja",
    "overview": "Every restaurant's stamp card is a crate label reduced to its essentials: flat ink, one heavy headline face, and a round seal inked for every visit. The system is a few opaque inks (ultramarine, tomato, canary, near-black) on a flat ultramarine ground, with generous radii, 1px hairline borders and soft shadows that lift cards off the page. There are no rays, ribbons, paper texture or text shadows.\n\nTwo registers coexist. Presentation surfaces (the home page, the customer join and card, the scanner) sit on the flat ultramarine ground and carry white, canary or deep-blue panes. Work surfaces (admin, dashboard, forms) sit on \"mesa\", a cool near-white, with white hairline-bordered controls. The scalloped star seal survives only as the wordmark. Label stock cream (papel) is no longer a UI surface; it is the background of the \"Etiqueta\" restaurant template and nothing else.\n\nThe restaurant's own colors lead on the card; the interface checks their contrast and corrects ink rather than trusting them.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Flat opaque inks; depth from two soft shadows and hairlines, never glows or hard offsets.",
      "Bricolage Grotesque 800 with tight tracking for names, headlines and numerals; Geist for everything read.",
      "Large radii: 12px controls, 16px panels, 24px cards and panes.",
      "The round check seal is the unit of progress, backed by a progress bar in the stamp card.",
      "One motion idea: the seal lands off-register and settles. Everything else is a single rise-in."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Green Is Success Rule",
        "body": "Leaf green never decorates; it means done.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Corrected Ink Rule",
        "body": "Restaurant colors are data. Text must reach 4.5:1 and seal ink 3:1 against the card ground, else the UI substitutes keyline ink or white (see readableOn).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Stock Rule",
        "body": "Cream is a restaurant template, not chrome. Never use it as a page, panel or pane background.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Headline Face Is For Names Rule",
        "body": "Bricolage Grotesque carries the restaurant name, page titles, pane titles and numerals. Never body copy, never buttons or form text.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Sentence Case Rule",
        "body": "Labels are sentence case, semibold, untracked. No uppercase tracked small print.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Soft Lift Rule",
        "body": "Only two shadows exist. Use them as written; never invent a third or add a hard offset.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do put one tomato (or, on ultramarine, one canary) action per view.",
      "Do show progress as round seals filled in order, with the progress bar beneath.",
      "Do run all restaurant colors through the contrast guard before painting text or seals.",
      "Do keep motion to the stamp landing and the rise-in, and honor prefers-reduced-motion (animations off, transitions near-zero).",
      "Do use mesa and white fields on work surfaces."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use a dark-slate wallet card with gold stars; that is the refused alternative.",
      "Don't add sunburst rays, ribbon banners, paper texture, text shadows or scalloped seals to the UI; the scalloped seal is the wordmark only.",
      "Don't set body copy, buttons or form text in Bricolage Grotesque.",
      "Don't use uppercase or letter-spaced labels.",
      "Don't use leaf green or amber as decoration.",
      "Don't use label cream as a page or panel background."
    ]
  }
}